- op: discuss  # 6c90
  at: '2026-09-16T08:06:10Z'  # 6c90
  by: agent  # 6c90
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6c90
  clock: 5  # 6c90
  nonce: 6c90  # 6c90
  message: "DONE 2026-09-16 by PR #998. Both halves shipped. Validated by running the\nthing, not by reading it.\n\nFIX (a). All five sites now share one definition and --full collects the\nrepo-root tests/. Measured on the merged tree:\n\n    ./scripts/smart-test --full\n    29,065 passed, 8 skipped, 15 xfailed in 1295s, coverage 100% (69,873 stmts)\n\nagainst the 26,702 this item records for the local run that went green while\nCI went red.\n\nTHE ITEM FILED THREE SITES; THERE WERE FIVE. The three came from grepping\nrun_pytest. kick_off_background_suite calls pytest directly, and\nrun_full_suite's MANIFEST WRITER enumerates with find -- so the full-suite\nMANIFEST, which is what CI consumes, also omitted root tests. Both were\ninvisible to that grep. The structural test that now forbids a packages-only\nsuite target outside the definition is the control that would have found them.\n\nTHE REASON THIS ITEM GAVE FOR DEFERRING (a) IS REFUTED. The 2026-09-14 entry\nrecords the risk as COV_PATHS -- \"root tests are not necessarily under\nCOV_PATHS, so adding them could turn a green --full red for reasons that have\nnothing to do with this defect\". Coverage is measured over packages/*/src, so\nmore tests can only RAISE it, and the combined run went green at 100%.\n\nTHE REAL BLOCKER WAS A BASENAME COLLISION, and it is why this could not have\nbeen a one-line change. tests/test_generate_concepts.py and\npackages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_generate_concepts.py share a basename;\nunder pytest's default prepend import mode, collecting both in one run raises\n\n    import file mismatch: imported module 'test_generate_concepts' has this\n    __file__ attribute: .../tests/test_generate_concepts.py\n\nand INTERRUPTS COLLECTION -- the run stops, it does not skip a file. Verified\nwith --collect-only before changing anything. The core file is renamed to match\nits own docstring (consumer-detection regexes), and a guard test now keeps the\ntwo suites' basenames disjoint, because a collision is cheap to fix when the\nfile is added and expensive to find later: the error names an import, not a\npolicy.\n\nFIX (b) shipped too, and the item was right that it is worth having\nindependently. The suite prints what it collected and what it did not, derived\nby asking git for tracked files and pyproject.toml for pytest's own\npython_files, so a seventh test directory is disclosed by the commit that adds\nit. On its first real run it reported .agent/hooks/_shared and a package src/\ntree as uncollected; both were FALSE POSITIVES of a glob looser than pytest's\nrule -- top_level_test_map.py, test_masking.py and test_only_reachability.py\nare production modules whose names merely contain the word. A disclosure that\ncries wolf on every run is a disclosure nobody reads.\n\nONE MORE, WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE bash -n MISSED IT. The disclosure's first cut\ncaptured its heredoc with report=$(...). It passed bash -n and died at RUNTIME\nwith \"syntax error near unexpected token ||\": a ) at column 0 inside the Python\nbody ends the command substitution early, and $() is re-parsed lazily so the\nstatic check never sees it. The behavioural test caught it, which is why that\ntest drives the function from a file instead of reading the script's text."  # 6c90
